H_D = [
"Evaluate the role of different modes of transport in India's economy and their problems. @ Roads carry most traffic in India, give door-to-door service and link villages, but suffer from congestion and accidents @ Railways are the principal mode for bulky goods and passengers over long distances, but face ticketless travel, theft, old tracks and congestion @ Pipelines carry crude oil and gas continuously at low cost, and waterways are the cheapest for heavy goods but are under-used because of silting and few terminals @ Air transport is the fastest and reaches remote areas through services such as Pawan Hans, but is costly @ Together they link production, consumption and trade, and each needs better maintenance and integration",
"Analyse the importance of ports and international trade for India. @ Ports are gateways of foreign trade: Mumbai, Kandla, Jawaharlal Nehru Port, Marmagao and Kochi on the west coast, and Chennai, Visakhapatnam, Paradip and Kolkata-Haldia on the east @ Mumbai has a spacious natural harbour, Kandla is a tidal port, Chennai is an artificial harbour and Visakhapatnam is the deepest landlocked and well-protected port @ Jawaharlal Nehru Port was built to decongest Mumbai @ International trade lets India exchange surplus goods for what it lacks and earn foreign exchange, exporting gems and jewellery, chemicals, engineering goods and software @ It imports petroleum, gold and machinery, and a favourable balance of trade strengthens the economy",
"Discuss the importance of communication in modern India. @ Communication spreads information, news and ideas quickly, and personal communication such as letters and telephones is complemented by mass communication such as radio, TV and newspapers @ India's postal network is the largest in the world and reaches remote villages @ Telecom links businesses, banks and markets, and ICT created services like call centres @ It supports education, health and governance and reduces isolation of remote areas @ Faster information flow aids trade and national integration",
"Critically examine road development in India from Nagpur Plan to NHAI. @ The Nagpur Plan of 1943 was the first planned programme of road development @ National highways expanded under the Central Public Works Department, and later the NHAI built the Golden Quadrilateral of 5,846 km linking Delhi, Kolkata, Chennai and Mumbai @ The North-South corridor links Srinagar and Kanyakumari and the East-West corridor links Silchar and Porbandar @ State highways, district roads and rural roads under PMGSY, and border roads by the BRO (formed in 1960), complete the network @ Challenges include congestion, accidents, poor maintenance and low road density in hilly states",
"Compare transport modes on cost, speed and suitability. @ Road transport is cheapest and most flexible for short distances and gives door-to-door service, but cost rises with distance and capacity is limited @ Rail is economical for bulky goods and long journeys but cannot reach everywhere and needs heavy investment @ Waterways are the cheapest for heavy goods and use less fuel, but are slow and depend on depth and season @ Air transport is the fastest and reaches difficult areas but is costly @ Pipelines suit liquids and gases, giving continuous low-cost flow without traffic",
"Examine the contribution of tourism to the Indian economy. @ Tourism earns foreign exchange and creates jobs in hotels, transport and handicrafts @ It promotes national integration and awareness of cultural heritage @ India offers heritage tourism, such as the forts and palaces of Rajasthan, and backwater tourism in Kerala @ It supports crafts and local economies @ It needs infrastructure and planning, otherwise it harms the environment",
"Explain how trade, transport and communication reinforce each other. @ Transport carries goods from producers to consumers and communication links buyers and sellers @ Trade needs transport; growing trade increases demand for roads, rails and ports @ Communication provides information on prices and orders @ Each helps the others grow and strengthens the economy @ Weakness in one reduces the effectiveness of the others",
"Assess the challenges of inland water transport and how to overcome them. @ Rivers are shallow, seasonal and silted, so navigation is difficult @ Terminals and equipment are few, and speed is slow @ National Waterways NW-1 on the Ganga from Haldia to Prayagraj, NW-2 on the Brahmaputra and NW-3 on the West Coast Canal are the main ones @ Dredging, modern terminals and cargo planning can improve use @ Policy support and integration with rail and road would raise their share",
"Discuss the role of pipelines and railways in energy and goods movement. @ Pipelines carry crude oil from oilfields to refineries, as from Assam to Barauni, and natural gas through the Hazira-Vijaipur-Jagdishpur line @ They provide continuous flow at low running cost without loading and unloading @ Railways carry coal, ore and goods in bulk to power stations and industries @ Both reduce load on roads and support industry @ Together they secure energy supply and goods movement",
"Evaluate the impact of globalisation on India's trade and transport. @ Globalisation raised trade volumes, so ports, roads and railways needed upgrading @ Software and services exports grew with communication technology @ Imports of oil and machinery rose @ Container ports like Jawaharlal Nehru Port handle growing cargo @ Competition forced improvement of transport and logistics",
"Explain regional disparities in transport infrastructure. @ Some regions such as the northern plains and coastal states have dense road and rail networks, while hilly and north-eastern areas have low density @ Kerala has high road density because of dense population, while Jammu and Kashmir has low density because of rugged terrain @ Rural areas lack all-weather roads @ Terrain and cost explain much of the difference @ Policy should extend connectivity to lagging regions through schemes like PMGSY",
"Discuss the environmental impact of transport and solutions. @ Vehicles burn fuel and pollute the air, and roads and railways cut through forests @ Noise, congestion and accidents increase @ Oil transport can cause spills @ Solutions include public transport, CNG, more rail and waterway use @ Cleaner fuels and efficient planning reduce the harm",
]
